><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20250701135230+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="9b3a360f-2982-45d6-8efa-33dfdde0dd08" date="D:20250701135230+10'00'" name="525b5863-ae16-4f9a-9c06-ca6407d6cdb8" icon="Comment" page="8" rect="41.551117,209.249893,65.551117,233.249893" subject="Sticky Note" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>TagedPCompression tights were the most effective garment for&#xD;increasing markers of venous return and muscle blood flow in&#xD;the lower limbs at rest, although compression shorts and&#xD;compression socks also had positive effects. These improvements&#xD;in blood flow were coupled with an improvement in&#xD;muscle oxygenation for compression tights and socks.&#xD;Manufacturers’ guidelines were appropriate for most participants&#xD;in exerting pressures required to alter measures of blood&#xD;flow. Therefore, sports compression garments may be a practical&#xD;intervention for enhancing markers of venous return and&#xD;muscle blood flow in the lower limbs while at rest.TagedEnd</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="8" rect="595.276001,141.249908,779.276001,233.249908"
/></text
></annots
><f href="10.1016_j.jshs.2021.07.010.pdf"
/><ids original="12E957393F46F734FC0AE34B1CDD9FCF" modified="BFEE1C719E3F4C4E8D29661E99951B4A"
